  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "The lines $y=\\frac{2}{5}x-2$ and $y=mx+b$ are perpendicular. If the line $y=mx+b$ passes through the point $(6,-1),$ what is the value of $b?$\n\n$b=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "Let ${l}_{1}$ denote the line $y=\\frac{2}{5}x-2,$ and let ${l}_{2}$ denote the line we want to find.\n\nSince perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other, the slope of ${l}_{2}$ is the negative reciprocal of the slope of ${l}_{1}.$ Since ${l}_{1}$ has a slope of $\\frac{2}{5},$ the line ${l}_{2}$ must have a slope of $-\\frac{5}{2}.$\n\nOur perpendicular line must have the slope $m=-\\frac{5}{2}$ and pass through the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(6,-1).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-1) & =-\\frac{5}{2}(x-6) \\\\ y+1 & =-\\frac{5}{2}x+15 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{5}{2}x+14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $b=14.$",
